{{wikipedia|Tweedledum and Tweedledee}}
==English==

===Etymology===
The names "Tweedledum" and "Tweedledee" first appeared in print in one of [[w:John Byrom|John Byrom]]'s epigrams, which satirised the disagreements between [[w:George Frideric Handel|Handel]] and [[w:Giovanni Battista Bononcini|Bononcini]]. They were made popular by [[w:Lewis Carroll|Lewis Carroll's]] ''[[w:Alice in Wonderland|Alice in Wonderland]]''.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One of a pair (the other of the pair being ''[[Tweedle-dee]]'') of nominally different but practically identical things.
#* '''1773''', “Epigram on the Feuds between Handel and Bononcini”, in ''Miscellaneous Poems'', Manchester: J. Harrop, [http://books.google.ca/books?id=OmogAAAAMAAJ&q=tweedle-dum#v=snippet&q=tweedle-dum&f=false pp 343–44]:
#*: Some ſay, compar’d to Bononcini, / That Mynheer Handel’s but a Ninny; / Others aver, that he to Handel / Is ſcarcely fit to hold a Candle: / Strange all this Difference ſhould be, / ’Twixt '''Tweedle-dum''' and Tweedle-dee!
#*{{quote-journal|journal=The Medical world|volume=25‎|page=216|year=1907|passage=How can we get the '''tweedle-dums''' and the tweedle-dees together for united action in the interests of the masses of the people?}}
#*{{quote-book|title=The king's equal‎|page=13|author=Katherine Paterson|year=1999|passage="She can't tell a '''Tweedle-dum''' from a Tweedle-dee."}}

====Quotations====
* '''2000''', Michelle Spring, ''Running for Shelter'', page 167:
*: '''Tweedledum’'''s chum stood with his hand resting lightly on the back of her arm, as if to turn her away from some potentially corrupting sight. [...] I stood my ground, smiled, and introduced myself. "I'm looking for Timothy Butler. That must be you." [...] I calmed down enough to notice that Timothy Butler was clenching and unclenching his right hand in an effort at self-control. '''Tweedledum''' noticed it too. She rushed to his defense.

